title: 'sc-keystore: fix panic and filename overflow in BLS381 paired-key generation'

doc:
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |
    Fixes two defects in filesystem-backed `LocalKeystore` when generating BLS381 /
    ECDSA_BLS381 paired keys, and unifies the on-disk key file format:

    - `ecdsa_bls381_generate_new` no longer panics when called with `seed = None`; the
      generated seed is now looked up through `key_phrase_by_type`, which handles the
      on-disk path.
    - All keys are now stored in a `.json` format: the filename is
      `hex(key_type || blake2_256(public_key))` with a `.json` suffix, and the body is a
      JSON object `{"public_key": <hex>, "phrase": <phrase>}`. This also resolves the
      255-byte filename limit for BLS381 / ECDSA_BLS381 public keys.

      As such a name no longer carries the public key. Legacy plain key files
      (`hex(key_type || public_key)` with a bare JSON-encoded phrase) are still read
      as a fallback but are no longer written, so existing keystores and tooling
      (e.g. Zombienet) that inject them remain unaffected. A deprecation warning is
      logged on startup for each legacy plain key file found in the keystore.
    - When the legacy plain format is deprecated, `open` should atomically migrate
      each legacy plain file to `.json`: write the `.json` file, and delete the plain
      file only if the phrases match.

crates:
- name: sc-keystore
  bump: patch
